<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-trapezoidal-rule" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Trapezoidal Rule</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.0 Measurements and Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p component="core">
                        2.2 Area Approximations
                    </p>
                    <p component="essential">
                        2.1 Area Approximations
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p component="core">
                        Determine area bounded by a curve and lines using the trapezoidal rule
                    </p>
                    <p component="essential">
                        Use trapezoidal rule to estimate area bounded by a curve and lines
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        2 lessons of 40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Content summary: Trapezoidal rule: area under a curve ≈ ½h[y₀ + yₙ + 2(y₁ + … + yₙ₋₁)].
         Suggested activity: Learners estimate the area under a curve such as y = x² + 1 using strips of equal width and the trapezoidal rule.
         Interactive (JSXGraph): Curve with a slider for the number of strips n; trapezia are drawn under the curve and the estimated area is shown next to the exact value. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLOs: Core (b), Essential (b). Lessons 2.1 and 2.2 as subsections.
         Activity: as planning notes (a curve with strips and the trapezoidal rule) | changed: curve y = x^2 + 2x + 3; Core derives the rule, Essential checks the given rule
         JSXGraph: fig-trapezoidal-strips-interactive (assets/jsxgraph/trapezoidal-strips.js)
         STACK: assets/stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/07-area-approximations/02-trapezoidal-rule/ -->

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 2.1 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-the-trapezoidal-rule">
        <title component="core">Deriving the Trapezoidal Rule</title>
        <title component="essential">Using the Trapezoidal Rule</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> a region under a curve can be cut into strips of equal width; <m>n</m> strips need <m>n + 1</m> ordinates; each strip can be treated as a trapezium.</p></li>
                <li component="core"><p><alert>Know (Core):</alert> why the inner ordinates are counted twice in the trapezoidal rule.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> use the trapezoidal rule with a table of measurements and with the equation of a curve.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> estimate the area of a plot measured with offsets from a straight fence.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p component="core">Learners replace each strip under a curve by a trapezium and add the areas; pairs then collect the terms into the trapezoidal rule.</p><p component="essential">Learners replace each strip under a curve by a trapezium and add the areas; pairs then check the given rule on the same numbers.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p component="core">First recall the area of a trapezium, <m>\tfrac{1}{2}(a + b)h</m>, from Grade 10. After part (c), ask <q>Which heights did you use twice?</q></p><p component="essential">First recall the area of a trapezium, <m>\tfrac{1}{2}(a + b)h</m>, from Grade 10. Show the rule in the box only after part (c).</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-trapezia-under-a-curve">
            <title>Trapezia Under a Curve</title>
            <statement>
                <p>The region under the curve <m>y = x^2 + 2x + 3</m> from <m>x = 0</m> to <m>x = 4</m> is cut into four strips, each <m>1</m> unit wide. In each strip the curve is replaced by a straight line joining its two ends, so each strip becomes a trapezium.</p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-trapezia-under-curve">
                    <caption>The region under <m>y = x^2 + 2x + 3</m> from <m>x = 0</m> to <m>x = 4</m> cut into four trapezia.</caption>
                    <image width="70%">
                        <shortdescription>The curve y = x squared plus 2x plus 3 from x = 0 to 4, cut into four strips of width 1. Each strip is a trapezium with a straight top. The vertical sides have heights y0 = 3, y1 = 6, y2 = 11, y3 = 18 and y4 = 27.</shortdescription>
                        <description><p>Horizontal axis x from 0 to 4, vertical axis y from 0 to 27. The curve y = x squared plus 2x plus 3 rises from 3 to 27. Vertical lines at x = 1, 2, 3 and 4 have heights 6, 11, 18 and 27; the height at x = 0 is 3. Straight lines join the tops of neighbouring vertical lines and lie just above the curve.</p></description>
                        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[width=9cm, height=7.5cm, xmin=0, xmax=5, ymin=0, ymax=30,
  axis lines=left, xtick={0,1,2,3,4}, ytick={0,10,20}, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}]
\fill[gray!25] (axis cs:0,0) -- (axis cs:0,3) -- (axis cs:1,6) -- (axis cs:2,11) -- (axis cs:3,18) -- (axis cs:4,27) -- (axis cs:4,0) -- cycle;
\draw (axis cs:0,3) -- (axis cs:1,6) -- (axis cs:2,11) -- (axis cs:3,18) -- (axis cs:4,27);
\draw (axis cs:1,0) -- (axis cs:1,6);
\draw (axis cs:2,0) -- (axis cs:2,11);
\draw (axis cs:3,0) -- (axis cs:3,18);
\draw (axis cs:4,0) -- (axis cs:4,27);
\addplot[very thick, domain=0:4, samples=60] {x^2+2*x+3};
\node[anchor=west, font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:0.03,1.5) {$y_0 = 3$};
\node[anchor=west, font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:1.03,3) {$y_1 = 6$};
\node[anchor=west, font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:2.03,5.5) {$y_2 = 11$};
\node[anchor=west, font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:3.03,9) {$y_3 = 18$};
\node[anchor=west, font=\small] at (axis cs:4.03,13.5) {$y_4 = 27$};
\node[font=\small] at (axis cs:1.5,22) {$y = x^2 + 2x + 3$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Copy and complete the table of values of <m>y = x^2 + 2x + 3</m> for <m>x = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4</m>.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>y = 3, 6, 11, 18, 27</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Find the area of each strip as a trapezium.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>\tfrac{1}{2} \times 1 \times (3 + 6) = 4.5</m>, then <m>8.5</m>, <m>14.5</m> and <m>22.5</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Add the four areas to estimate the area of the region.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>4.5 + 8.5 + 14.5 + 22.5 = 50</m> square units.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li component="core">
                            <p>Work in pairs. Call the strip width <m>h</m> and the heights <m>y_0, y_1, y_2, y_3, y_4</m>. Write the area of each trapezium using these letters, add the four areas and collect the terms. Which heights appear twice?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>\tfrac{1}{2}h(y_0 + y_1) + \tfrac{1}{2}h(y_1 + y_2) + \tfrac{1}{2}h(y_2 + y_3) + \tfrac{1}{2}h(y_3 + y_4) = \tfrac{1}{2}h[y_0 + y_4 + 2(y_1 + y_2 + y_3)]</m>. The inner heights <m>y_1, y_2, y_3</m> appear twice: each is a side of two trapezia.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li component="essential">
                            <p>Work in pairs. Use the trapezoidal rule in the box after this activity with <m>h = 1</m> and check that you get the same answer as in part (c).</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>\tfrac{1}{2} \times 1 \times [3 + 27 + 2(6 + 11 + 18)] = \tfrac{1}{2} \times 100 = 50</m>, the same as part (c).</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>The exact area of the region is <m>49\tfrac{1}{3}</m>. Use the interactive in <xref ref="fig-trapezoidal-strips-interactive"/> with <m>8</m> strips. Is the estimate closer?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>With <m>8</m> strips the estimate is <m>49.5</m>, closer to <m>49\tfrac{1}{3}</m> than <m>50</m> is. Narrower strips hug the curve more closely.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p component="core">To state the trapezoidal rule from the pairs' own working, and the steps for using it.</p><p component="essential">To state the trapezoidal rule and the steps for using it.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> the four trapezia of part (b) are the strips; their vertical sides are the ordinates.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The strip width is <m>h = (b - a) \div n</m>. There are <m>n + 1</m> ordinates for <m>n</m> strips.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The rule in words: first plus last plus twice the rest, then multiply by <m>\tfrac{1}{2}h</m>.</p></li>
                    <li component="core"><p>Use the pairs' answers to part (d): the inner ordinates appear twice because each is shared by two trapezia.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-trapezoidal-rule-core" component="core">
            <title>Deriving the Trapezoidal Rule</title>
            <p>
                To estimate the area bounded by a curve, the <m>x</m>-axis and the lines <m>x = a</m> and <m>x = b</m>, cut the region into <m>n</m> <term>strips</term> of equal width
                <md>h = \frac{b - a}{n}</md>.
                The heights of the curve at the edges of the strips are the <term>ordinates</term> <m>y_0, y_1, \ldots, y_n</m>, at <m>x_0 = a</m>, <m>x_1 = a + h</m>, and so on up to <m>x_n = b</m>. There are <m>n + 1</m> ordinates: the strips are the gaps between them.
            </p>
            <p>
                In general, strip <m>k</m> lies between the ordinates <m>y_{k-1}</m> and <m>y_k</m>. Replace the curve over the strip by the straight line, or <term>chord</term>, joining its two ends. The strip becomes a trapezium of area <m>\tfrac{1}{2}h(y_{k-1} + y_k)</m>. Adding the <m>n</m> trapezia,
                <md>
                    <mrow>A \amp \approx \tfrac{1}{2}h(y_0 + y_1) + \tfrac{1}{2}h(y_1 + y_2) + \cdots + \tfrac{1}{2}h(y_{n-1} + y_n)</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = \tfrac{1}{2}h\,[y_0 + y_1 + y_1 + y_2 + \cdots + y_{n-1} + y_{n-1} + y_n]</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = \tfrac{1}{2}h\,[y_0 + y_n + 2(y_1 + y_2 + \cdots + y_{n-1})]</mrow>
                </md>.
            </p>
            <p>
                This is the <term>trapezoidal rule</term>: half the strip width times the first plus the last plus twice the rest. Each inner ordinate is a side of two trapezia, so it is counted twice.
            </p>
        </insight>

        <insight xml:id="insight-trapezoidal-rule-essential" component="essential">
            <title>Using the Trapezoidal Rule</title>
            <p>
                To estimate the area bounded by a curve, the <m>x</m>-axis and the lines <m>x = a</m> and <m>x = b</m>, cut the region into <m>n</m> <term>strips</term> of equal width <m>h</m>. The heights of the curve at the edges of the strips are the <term>ordinates</term> <m>y_0, y_1, \ldots, y_n</m>. There are <m>n + 1</m> ordinates: the strips are the gaps between them. Each strip is treated as a trapezium whose top is the straight line, or <term>chord</term>, joining two neighbouring points on the curve. The <term>trapezoidal rule</term> is
                <md>A \approx \tfrac{1}{2}h\,[y_0 + y_n + 2(y_1 + y_2 + \cdots + y_{n-1})]</md>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="1.">
                    <li><p>Find the strip width: <m>h = (b - a) \div n</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Make a table of the ordinates <m>y_0, y_1, \ldots, y_n</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Work out the first plus the last plus <m>2 \times</m> (the sum of the others).</p></li>
                    <li><p>Multiply by <m>\tfrac{1}{2}h</m>.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
            <p>
                Each inner ordinate is a side of two trapezia, so it is counted twice.
            </p>
        </insight>

        <figure xml:id="fig-trapezoidal-strips-interactive">
            <caption>Drag the slider to change the number of strips. The trapezia and the estimate update; the exact area is <m>49.33</m>.</caption>
            <interactive xml:id="interactive-trapezoidal-strips" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="16:12" source="jsxgraph/trapezoidal-strips.js">
                <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-trapezoidal-strips" surface="jsxboard" aspect="16:12"/>
                <instructions>
                    <p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><p>The curve is <m>y = x^2 + 2x + 3</m> from <m>x = 0</m> to <m>x = 4</m>, as in <xref ref="act-trapezia-under-a-curve"/>.</p></li>
                            <li><p>Drag the <alert>n</alert> slider from <m>1</m> to <m>12</m>. Check that <m>1</m> strip gives <m>60</m>, <m>2</m> strips give <m>52</m>, <m>4</m> give <m>50</m>, <m>8</m> give <m>49.5</m> and <m>12</m> give <m>49.41</m>.</p></li>
                            <li><p>Is the estimate ever below the exact area? Why do narrower strips give a better estimate?</p></li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </instructions>
            </interactive>
        </figure>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-offsets-from-a-fence">
            <title>Offsets From a Fence</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Wanjiku measured a plot beside a road near Nyeri. Along a straight fence <m>60\,\text{m}</m> long she measured the distance from the fence to the curved edge of the plot (the <term>offset</term>) every <m>10\,\text{m}</m>. Use the trapezoidal rule to estimate the area of the plot.</p>
                <table xml:id="table-offsets-from-a-fence">
                    <title>Offsets from the Fence</title>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Distance along fence (m)</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>10</cell><cell>20</cell><cell>30</cell><cell>40</cell><cell>50</cell><cell>60</cell></row>
                        <row><cell>Offset (m)</cell><cell>32</cell><cell>35</cell><cell>37</cell><cell>36</cell><cell>33</cell><cell>29</cell><cell>24</cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>There are <m>7</m> offsets, so there are <m>6</m> strips, each <m>h = 10\,\text{m}</m> wide. The first and last offsets are <m>32</m> and <m>24</m>; the inner ones add to <m>35 + 37 + 36 + 33 + 29 = 170</m>.
                    <md>A \approx \tfrac{1}{2} \times 10 \times [32 + 24 + 2 \times 170] = 5 \times 396 = 1980</md>
                    The plot is about <m>1980\,\text{m}^2</m>. Note that <m>7</m> measurements make <m>6</m> strips.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-strips-two-units-wide">
            <title>Strips Two Units Wide</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Use the trapezoidal rule with <m>3</m> strips to estimate the area bounded by the curve <m>y = 8 + 2x - \tfrac{1}{2}x^2</m>, the <m>x</m>-axis and the lines <m>x = 0</m> and <m>x = 6</m>. Then repeat with <m>6</m> strips.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>With <m>3</m> strips, <m>h = (6 - 0) \div 3 = 2</m>, so the ordinates are at <m>x = 0, 2, 4, 6</m>:</p>
                <table xml:id="table-strips-two-units-wide">
                    <title>Ordinates with <m>h = 2</m></title>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell><m>x</m></cell><cell>0</cell><cell>2</cell><cell>4</cell><cell>6</cell></row>
                        <row><cell><m>y</m></cell><cell>8</cell><cell>10</cell><cell>8</cell><cell>2</cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <p><md>A \approx \tfrac{1}{2} \times 2 \times [8 + 2 + 2(10 + 8)] = 1 \times 46 = 46</md>
                    With <m>6</m> strips, <m>h = 1</m> and the ordinates at <m>x = 0, 1, \ldots, 6</m> are <m>8, 9.5, 10, 9.5, 8, 5.5, 2</m>:
                    <md>A \approx \tfrac{1}{2} \times 1 \times [8 + 2 + 2(9.5 + 10 + 9.5 + 8 + 5.5)] = \tfrac{1}{2} \times 95 = 47.5</md></p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-weight-column">
            <title>A Weight Column</title>
            <statement>
                <p>The width of a flower bed was measured every <m>2\,\text{m}</m> along a straight path. The widths, in metres, were <m>3.0, 4.2, 4.8, 4.6, 3.8, 2.4</m>. Estimate the area of the flower bed with the trapezoidal rule.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Give each width a <q>weight</q>: <m>1</m> for the first and last, <m>2</m> for the others. Then add the products.</p>
                <table xml:id="table-weight-column">
                    <title>The Trapezoidal Rule as a Table</title>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Distance <m>x</m> (m)</cell><cell>Width <m>y</m> (m)</cell><cell>Weight</cell><cell>Weight <m>\times</m> <m>y</m></cell></row>
                        <row><cell>0</cell><cell>3.0</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>3.0</cell></row>
                        <row><cell>2</cell><cell>4.2</cell><cell>2</cell><cell>8.4</cell></row>
                        <row><cell>4</cell><cell>4.8</cell><cell>2</cell><cell>9.6</cell></row>
                        <row><cell>6</cell><cell>4.6</cell><cell>2</cell><cell>9.2</cell></row>
                        <row><cell>8</cell><cell>3.8</cell><cell>2</cell><cell>7.6</cell></row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>10</cell><cell>2.4</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>2.4</cell></row>
                        <row><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell>Total</cell><cell>40.2</cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <p><md>A \approx \tfrac{1}{2} \times 2 \times 40.2 = 40.2</md>
                    The flower bed is about <m>40.2\,\text{m}^2</m>. The weight column is the trapezoidal rule written as a table; it is also how a spreadsheet does the calculation.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type decimals with a point, for example <c>37.6</c>. Give exact answers unless the question asks you to round.</p>

